.TH findneeded 1
.SH NAME
findneeded \- List the ports a package links to directly, as a row.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.B findneeded
.I package
.SH DESCRIPTION

The \fBfindneeded\fP script outputs the ports whose libraries an installed
package links to directly, as a single row, suitable for the \fB# Depends on:\fP
line in the \fBPkgfile\fP(5). Dependencies from \fB(core)\fP are omitted.
Only one package is checked at a time.

Unlike \fBfinddeps\fP(1), which uses \fBldd\fP(1) and reports every library that
gets loaded (including the libraries of libraries), this tool reads only the
libraries the package's own files ask for, the NEEDED entries shown by
\fBreadelf\fP(1). Each library is matched to the package that provides it in the
standard library folders, as found by \fBldconfig\fP(8) and \fBpkginfo\fP(8).
Copies bundled by other packages in private folders are ignored.

Libraries that no installed package provides are reported as \fBnot found\fP.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
no options available

.SH EXAMPLES
.B
findneeded lxappearance
.PP
displays the ports that lxappearance binaries and libs link to directly:

.PP
at-spi2-core cairo gdk-pixbuf glib gtk3 harfbuzz pango xorg-libx11

.B
NOTE
.PP
Some of the listed ports may come with other dependencies in the list (here
most of them come with gtk3). Run \fBfindredundantdeps\fP(1) afterwards to
trim them.

For prebuilt programs that bundle their own libraries, use \fBfinddeps\fP(1)
instead. Build-time tools (e.g. intltool, which lxappearance needs to build) and
programs a package only runs (e.g. openvpn for networkmanager-openvpn) cannot
be detected and have to be added by hand.

.SH SEE ALSO
\fBfinddeps\fP(1), \fBfinddepsrow\fP(1), \fBfindredundantdeps\fP(1), \fBreadelf\fP(1), \fBldconfig\fP(8), \fBpkginfo\fP(8), \fBprt\-get\fP(8), \fBPkgfile\fP(5)

